WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.150
CHESTER LUI: Hi, everyone.

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:08.580
Welcome to Cloud On Air, live
webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.500
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.530
And my name is
Chester Lui, and I'll

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.920
be talking about protecting
your workforce with Chrome

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.120
Enterprise.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.670
You could ask questions
anytime on this platform,

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.980
and we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.250
So this presentation
is really broken down

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.150
into three sections.

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:34.290
In the first section, we will
discuss some industry insights

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.380
and trends by
cybersecurity and show both

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.750
how businesses are impacted.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:43.110
In the second session, we'll
talk about the security

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.290
benefits of Chrome OS, bundled
with enterprise features

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.110
unlocked by the Chrome
Enterprise license that can

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:52.080
help your company stay safe.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.560
In the third and final section,
we'll take a look at Google

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.270
and how it keeps
its own business

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.730
secure from malicious threats.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.800
And with that,
let's get started.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.460
With the growing media reports
of state-sponsored espionage,

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.340
financial crime, and
tampering with elections

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.010
and other malicious
behaviors, cybercrime

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:17.790
has moved to the forefront
of the current news cycle.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.560
This has not only
become a concern

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.770
for individuals but for
businesses of all shapes

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.120
and sizes as well.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.890
We face new
challenges, and we have

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:28.960
to keep our businesses secure.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.950
Headlines like these not only
show well-publicized hacks

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.070
that have resulted in
significant losses,

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.770
they reiterate the
need for businesses

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.275
to revisit their
cybersecurity strategy.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:48.810
IT administrators have
more complexity to manage,

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.220
as business demands evolve.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.340
They have to defend
against external attacks

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.220
and internal vulnerabilities,
while keeping

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.960
the business moving.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.510
With the purveyance of
increasing affordability

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:04.410
of cloud computing, mobile
devices and connectivity,

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.180
technology and the
work environment

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.706
is vastly different
from what it used to be.

00:02:09.706 --> 00:02:11.039
On average-- let's take a look--

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:13.140
an employee typically
has three devices

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:17.130
to do their job, many of which
are not tethered to the desk

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:20.420
and can be used
outside of the office.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:24.890
The average employee also
has about 22 cloud apps

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.870
that are installed,
many of which

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:30.290
have not been approved
by IT administrators.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:35.240
As a result, security concerns
are greater than ever,

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:39.200
as there are more and more
points of attacks and leakage.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.650
98% of Fortune
500 companies were

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.950
affected by malware in 2017.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.590
And as a result of
that, 69% of those

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:49.730
were affected by phishing.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.660
So workers are also
highly targeted

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:59.420
for phishing and
malware attacks.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.700
Google has conducted
research and has

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:06.860
shown that enterprise users
are 6.2 times more likely to be

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:11.120
targeted for phishing
attacks, and 4.3 times more

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.080
likely to receive malware
than typical end users.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.530
Given the sheer rate of
enterprises and its employees

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.380
as targeted by phishing
and malware threats,

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.980
it's important to implement
a strategy that deters

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:26.840
falling prey to these attacks.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.470
Not only are cyber attacks
affecting proprietary company

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:38.550
data, but it's also becoming
a large financial burden.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.430
Current estimates show that the
global cost of data breaches

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:46.230
will reach $2.1 trillion
by the end of 2019,

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:50.220
according to the
Cybersecurity Ventures 2017

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.350
cybercrime report.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:56.250
Hackers are not only
looking to cash in on data,

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.740
they're collecting
account passwords.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:01.970
They're finding new ways
to hold data hostage.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:06.390
In attacks like ransomware,
data on end-user machines

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.940
are encrypted by
a malicious party,

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.260
only to be decrypted
with a payment.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:16.970
Ransomware attacks have
increased 15 times since 2015.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:19.640
Along with this,
employees continue

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:23.420
to be vulnerable to attacks,
which are increasing

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.520
and becoming more complex.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.490
According to the Willis
Towers Watson Cyber Risk

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:32.930
Survey in 2017,
90% of all reported

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:36.410
breaches were caused
by employee negligence.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:40.190
With the increasing number
of sophisticated back actors,

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:43.130
along with the growing numbers
of vulnerable employees,

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.470
these numbers could
continue to be on the rise.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:53.650
So with the shift targeting
increasing in complexity

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.240
amongst the cybersecurity
environment,

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.580
IT has to find solutions
and preventative measures

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:03.590
to handle the risks associated
with enterprise threats.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:09.580
A solution to consider for
this changing cybersecurity

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:12.880
landscape is Chrome Enterprise.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.120
Chrome Enterprise
provides solutions

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:18.520
fit for the workplace
of the future.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.310
We provide a secure,
consistent user experience

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:25.090
across a range of devices
that can be used anywhere

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:29.410
with Chrome OS and granular
controls and enterprise

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:32.200
features with the Chrome
Enterprise license.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:37.660
When it comes to security,
Chrome Enterprise,

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:41.920
follows three pillars
proactive protections,

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.670
evolving defenses,
and granular policies.

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:47.530
These three buckets
come together

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:50.290
to tell a really
compelling story

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:53.590
about how Chrome Enterprise
approaches security

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:55.120
in an entirely different way.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:01.710
Chrome OS follows a
multi-layered interlocking

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:06.060
approach to proactively
protecting data on the device.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:09.150
From the hardware to the
firmware to the operating

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:12.210
system to the applications
and ultimately,

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:16.350
the browser level, all
layers of the Chromebook

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:21.290
are working together to provide
a secure computing experience.

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:24.260
So, like I highlighted earlier,
starting at the hardware level,

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:28.070
every Chromebook comes with
a Google-designed hardware

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:31.160
security module that
is tamper resistant

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:34.660
and encrypts all the
data on the device.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:37.570
At the firmware
level, verify boot

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:40.090
ensures that the operating
system has not been tampered

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:43.180
with by an untrusted source.

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:45.100
Chromebooks are
unique because we

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:49.430
have two versions of Chrome
OS on the device at all times.

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.300
So if one version is detected
to have been tampered with,

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.430
the Chromebook will
attempt to boot

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:58.800
the other version of the
operating system on the device.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.130
Now let's take a look at
the operating system level.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:05.520
OS updates and patches
happen in the background

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.480
thus ensuring no downtime while
your employees are getting up

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:10.455
to date.

00:07:10.455 --> 00:07:12.330
Since there are two
versions of the operating

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.370
system on a
Chromebook, the version

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:16.680
that is not being
used by the end user

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.480
will be updated
in the background

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:21.810
to prevent interruption,
only to be booted up

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:24.450
upon the next machine start.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:29.250
No more employees pressing
ignore on update prompts.

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:32.700
Chrome OS also stores
most user data securely

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:37.420
encrypted in the cloud, rather
than locally on the device.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:41.070
This removes the need to protect
it on the local device thus

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.250
removing the threat.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:45.230
Lastly, at the
application level,

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:49.220
Google Play Protect ensures that
the applications distributed

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:52.400
in Google Play
are not malicious.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:55.990
We'll talk more
about this in a bit.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:59.220
So if you look at the
overall value proposition,

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:03.930
all of these benefits come
stock on Chrome OS devices

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:08.790
to ensure a highly secure
computing experience.

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:12.870
Chrome OS comes with Chrome
browser and all of its security

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:16.260
benefits, which come on all
devices with Chrome browser,

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:17.880
no matter the operating system.

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:27.070
In 2017, we had two
security engineering firms

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:30.610
develop two white papers to
examine the relative security

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:34.120
strengths of the three most
popular enterprise browsers,

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:36.970
Chrome browser, Microsoft
Edge, and Microsoft Internet

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:38.610
Explorer.

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:40.650
The research of these
firms have found

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:43.320
that Chrome browser
was able to detect

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:48.240
more phishing sites than
Edge, due to our Safe Browsing

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:49.830
technology--

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:53.550
actually, 83% to 76%.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.430
The research also determined
that Chrome browser

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.680
was the quickest of
the three browsers

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:01.110
to patch major security bugs.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:08.300
Next, I want to talk about
our solution against phishing

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:09.530
attacks.

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:13.940
Chromebooks, in conjunction
with security keys and Safe

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:17.270
Browsing, make G
Suite users secure

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:19.800
against these types of attacks.

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:26.310
First, security keys-- security
keys are supported by G Suite

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:29.100
and provide a second
requirement for authenticating

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:30.806
into your account.

00:09:30.806 --> 00:09:32.430
The security key is
a piece of hardware

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:35.610
that can be plugged
directly into, for example,

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.040
the USB report on
your device and ensure

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:42.850
that even if your password is
obtained by a malicious party,

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:46.770
they would still need the
security key to login.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:49.570
Secondly, let's talk
about Safe Browsing.

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:52.960
Safe Browsing warns users
of malicious threats

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.100
before they actually happen.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:58.870
So if a site is detected to be
a phishing site, for example,

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:03.780
the end user will be prompted
to steer clear and turn away.

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:08.610
Finally, and as discussed in
previous slides, Chromebooks

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.800
offer background updates.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:12.900
This ensures end
users are always

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:17.340
up-to-date with the patches and
the most recent vulnerability

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:20.890
patches onboard their
devices and the browser.

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:29.770
Next, let's discuss
Chrome Enterprise license.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.860
The Chrome Enterprise
license unlocks

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:35.320
a collection of
enterprise features

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:39.130
and granular management that
help IT administrators manage

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:44.110
a large fleet of devices
with over 200-plus policies.

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:47.410
All of this plugs into
a legacy infrastructure

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:50.290
and allows administrators
to deploy applications

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:52.720
to their end users.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:55.990
With userbase policies,
IT administrators

00:10:55.990 --> 00:11:00.190
can ensure that
employees are compliant,

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:04.370
regardless of which Chrome
device they are using.

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:07.970
With device-based
policies, IT administrators

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:11.240
can ensure devices adhere
to strict protocols,

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:15.990
regardless of which
user is accessing them.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:17.970
With Chrome
Enterprise licensing,

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:22.350
IT administrators can
also manage applications

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:27.240
and extensions and distribute
them in a curated list,

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:29.700
based on an end-user group.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:32.370
And lastly, with fleet
management commands,

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:37.080
IT administrators can
easily provision, monitor,

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:40.080
and lock down devices at
the touch of a button.

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:47.600
The Google Admin console is a
cloud-based device management

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:49.180
portal.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:52.660
This capability to manage
Chrome browser and OS

00:11:52.660 --> 00:11:55.660
started over six years ago.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:57.610
And we're even more
excited to announce

00:11:57.610 --> 00:12:01.030
that we are partnering with
third-party enterprise mobility

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:03.220
management providers
to give you an even

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:07.300
more flexible and broader,
easier access to security

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:09.490
features.

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:11.890
We just announced a partnership
with VMware AirWatch

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:16.510
last year, making them
the first third-party EMM

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:19.150
solution to manage Chromebooks.

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:21.610
We also just announced
new partnerships

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:27.630
with Cisco Meraki, Citrix
Xen Mobile, and IBM Mass 360,

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:30.440
and there are many more to come.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:35.930
We also have integrated Chrome
OS with Active Directory

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:39.380
to allow customers to enjoy
the benefits of Chrome

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:42.145
within their AD infrastructure.

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:48.050
For those of you
who are unaware,

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:52.410
select Chrome OS devices
support the Google Play Store,

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:56.850
bringing the most popular mobile
app ecosystem to Chromebooks.

00:12:56.850 --> 00:13:00.000
With Managed Google
Play, IT administrators

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.510
can curate application
lists for employees

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:05.775
and even have different
lists for different groups.

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:12.120
One of the major
benefits of using

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:16.650
Google Play as an application
distribution mechanism

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:21.070
is the malware detection that
happens on the server side.

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:24.640
Google Play has a feature
called Google Play Protect.

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:32.410
This offering scans over
50 billion apps every day

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:35.570
to ensure malware
is not detected.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:38.840
As a result of this,
over 2 billion devices

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:41.210
are protected by
Google Play Protect

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:46.185
by using applications
deemed to be safe.

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:53.890
Now to our final section.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:57.790
Let's take a look at how Google
thinks about application access

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:01.540
control and keeps itself secure.

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:03.940
When it comes to
protecting its data,

00:14:03.940 --> 00:14:08.930
Google is considered three
areas to ensure properly

00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:12.630
security measures that
are put into place.

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:15.600
The first bucket are users.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:17.700
We need to ensure that
the right users have

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:21.450
the right level of access
to get their jobs done.

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:24.480
The second bucket
is applications.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:27.660
Applications provide
access to data

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:31.870
that potentially not all
employees can have access to.

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:34.780
And the third bucket is devices.

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:37.540
Google's considers
this deeply when

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:41.200
determining when an
employee can have access.

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:43.630
What types of
devices are employees

00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:46.780
accessing from, and
also is the device

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:49.280
a corporate-issued device?

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:51.220
Google ultimately
needs a solution

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:53.245
for detecting this
type of scenario.

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:00.150
So when you combine
these three things,

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:04.500
you leverage a methodology
called Beyond Core, which

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:07.256
is essentially tiered access.

00:15:07.256 --> 00:15:10.140
In a tiered access
model, policies

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:13.020
are set at a corporate level.

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:17.730
So internal services are
classified by trusted tier.

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:20.040
User credentials,
for example, are

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:25.280
verified when resources requests
are made from specific devices.

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:29.300
Given this information, access
to data and applications

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:33.200
are granted if the risk profile
of the end user and device

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:36.320
matches the trust-tier.

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:40.210
Google has moved away from
perimeter-based security

00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:42.670
and IP-based trust.

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:46.060
What this gives
you is an employee

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:48.580
that could connect to the
internet from anywhere

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:52.870
in the world without a
VPN and be able to access

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:54.310
his or her applications.

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:02.160
There are three primary
Beyond Core principles.

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:05.360
The first one, connecting
from a particular network,

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:09.380
must not determine which
services you can access.

00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:14.300
The second is access to services
is granted based on what we

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:17.510
know about you and your device.

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:22.910
And the third, all access to
services must be authenticated,

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:25.840
authorized, and encrypted.

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:29.650
To provide a simplified
example, consider the use case

00:16:29.650 --> 00:16:32.230
of various employees at Google.

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:36.250
An employee may only
require a low tier of access

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:40.060
to retrieve access, for
example, to the cafeteria menu.

00:16:40.060 --> 00:16:43.070
They don't need access to
more sensitive services,

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:45.010
like the HR portal.

00:16:45.010 --> 00:16:48.160
Assigning the lowest
tier of access

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:53.000
required to complete a request
has several advantages.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:55.600
It decreases the maintenance
of cost associated

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:59.290
with highly secure
devices and also improves

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:01.830
the usability of the device.

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:05.310
As a device is allowed to
access more sensitive data,

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:10.359
we require more frequent tests
of user presence on the device.

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:13.290
So the more we trust
a given device,

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:15.863
the shorter-lived
its credentials.

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:27.060
So to wrap this up, there
are three major takeaways.

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:31.060
Endpoints are critical
to cloud security.

00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:35.180
More devices and applications
are being used by employees

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:39.150
from more locations
than ever before,

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:42.720
driving malicious actors with
more sophisticated hacks.

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:44.760
This creates a new
security paradigm

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:46.950
for businesses,
who ultimately need

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:52.120
the right strategy and tools
to keep their business safe.

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:57.680
Security at the browser, device,
and OS level is essential.

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:01.420
Chrome Enterprise provides
a secure entry point

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:05.410
to the cloud with Chrome OS
and the Enterprise features

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:08.440
and tools you need
to ensure employees

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:12.240
are compliant with the
Chrome Enterprise license.

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:16.170
And finally, trust
endpoints and apps

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:18.870
enable a tiered access strategy.

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:23.430
At Google, we focus on
a tiered access strategy

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:26.910
for users, applications,
and devices

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:30.240
to ensure data is secured
and always secure.

